WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.327
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:08.920
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:14.833
Unit one: The Nature of Truth. In this unit, you will examine how we know what is true.

4
00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.211
epistemology.

5
00:00:17.911 --> 00:00:23.417
epistemology means the branch of philosophy concerned with knowledge and belief.

6
00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:27.609
Epistemology asks how we can justify what we claim to know.

7
00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:31.948
Your turn. Say: epistemology.

8
00:00:34.648 --> 00:00:35.928
epistemology.

9
00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:38.022
relativism.

10
00:00:38.722 --> 00:00:45.203
relativism means the view that truth or morality depends on culture or perspective.

11
00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:49.987
Moral relativism makes universal human rights harder to defend.

12
00:00:50.687 --> 00:00:54.721
Your turn. Say: relativism.

13
00:00:57.421 --> 00:00:58.550
relativism.

14
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.155
verifiability.

15
00:01:01.855 --> 00:01:05.933
verifiability means the quality of being checkable against evidence.

16
00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:09.695
Scientific claims depend on their verifiability.

17
00:01:10.395 --> 00:01:13.837
Your turn. Say: verifiability.

18
00:01:16.537 --> 00:01:17.782
verifiability.

19
00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:19.854
post-truth.

20
00:01:20.554 --> 00:01:25.003
post-truth means describing a culture where emotion matters more than facts.

21
00:01:25.303 --> 00:01:28.615
Some commentators speak of a post-truth era.

22
00:01:29.315 --> 00:01:32.478
Your turn. Say: post-truth.

23
00:01:35.178 --> 00:01:36.249
post-truth.

24
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:38.100
dogma.

25
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.030
dogma means a belief presented as unquestionable.

26
00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:45.073
Science advances by challenging dogma.

27
00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:47.856
Your turn. Say: dogma.

28
00:01:50.556 --> 00:01:51.488
dogma.

29
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:53.682
certitude.

30
00:01:54.382 --> 00:01:56.869
certitude means absolute certainty.

31
00:01:57.169 --> 00:02:00.086
Few philosophers speak with such certitude.

32
00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:04.030
Your turn. Say: certitude.

33
00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:07.801
certitude.

34
00:02:08.817 --> 00:02:10.131
empiricism.

35
00:02:10.831 --> 00:02:15.687
empiricism means the view that knowledge comes from experience and observation.

36
00:02:15.987 --> 00:02:19.577
Empiricism underpins the scientific method.

37
00:02:20.277 --> 00:02:23.788
Your turn. Say: empiricism.

38
00:02:26.488 --> 00:02:27.640
empiricism.

39
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.628
rationalism.

40
00:02:30.328 --> 00:02:34.510
rationalism means the view that reason is the main source of knowledge.

41
00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.773
Descartes is a key figure in rationalism.

42
00:02:38.473 --> 00:02:41.625
Your turn. Say: rationalism.

43
00:02:44.325 --> 00:02:45.419
rationalism.

44
00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:48.579
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:52.562
Rithy, can we ever be absolutely certain that something is true?

46
00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:59.312
Rarely, I'd say. Only in mathematics do we come close to complete certitude.

47
00:03:00.617 --> 00:03:03.582
Interesting. So you lean towards empiricism?

48
00:03:04.883 --> 00:03:11.469
Largely, yes, though rationalism has its place: not all knowledge comes from the senses.

49
00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:17.967
And relativism? Some argue that truth is simply what a culture believes.

50
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.398
Were that so, scientific progress would be meaningless. Verifiability matters.

51
00:03:25.683 --> 00:03:29.076
Yet people now speak of a post-truth age.

52
00:03:30.383 --> 00:03:36.356
Not until we value evidence over emotion will that change. Dogma thrives on certainty without proof.

53
00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.577
Grammar focus: Advanced inversion and emphasis.

54
00:03:41.877 --> 00:03:46.779
At C2, inversion adds weight and elegance to key points.

55
00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:54.213
Only in mathematics do we come close to certainty. Not until we value evidence will that change.

56
00:03:54.513 --> 00:03:57.464
Little did they realise how fragile truth was.

57
00:03:57.764 --> 00:04:04.640
At C2, inversion is used sparingly but precisely, to give weight to a key point in speech or writing.

58
00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.279
Now read and listen. What Can We Know?.

59
00:04:08.579 --> 00:04:13.516
Epistemology, the study of knowledge, is among the oldest branches of philosophy.

60
00:04:14.817 --> 00:04:20.021
Rationalists such as Descartes argued that reason alone could establish certain truths.

61
00:04:21.317 --> 00:04:27.089
Empiricists such as Locke and Hume insisted that knowledge begins with experience.

62
00:04:28.383 --> 00:04:34.214
Modern science combines both traditions, testing reasoned hypotheses against observation.

63
00:04:35.517 --> 00:04:41.417
Relativism challenges this by suggesting that truth depends on cultural perspective.

64
00:04:42.717 --> 00:04:48.571
In what some call a post-truth era, emotional appeal often outweighs evidence in public debate.

65
00:04:49.883 --> 00:04:56.504
Only by valuing verifiability and intellectual humility can societies resist dogma.

66
00:04:57.817 --> 00:05:04.843
Perhaps the mark of an educated mind is not certitude, but the capacity for well-founded doubt.

67
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.930
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:11.810
The study of knowledge and belief is blank.

69
00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:16.309
A, epistemology. B, empiricism. C, dogma.

70
00:05:20.317 --> 00:05:23.094
The answer is A: epistemology.

71
00:05:24.583 --> 00:05:27.605
Only in mathematics blank complete certainty.

72
00:05:27.905 --> 00:05:32.684
A, we find. B, do we find. C, we do find.

73
00:05:36.683 --> 00:05:39.205
The answer is B: do we find.

74
00:05:40.717 --> 00:05:43.390
Not until he apologised blank the problem.

75
00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:50.629
A, she understood. B, did she understand. C, she did understand.

76
00:05:54.617 --> 00:05:57.603
The answer is B: did she understand.

77
00:05:59.117 --> 00:06:02.370
A belief presented as unquestionable is blank.

78
00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:07.217
A, dogma. B, verifiability. C, certitude.

79
00:06:11.217 --> 00:06:13.135
The answer is A: dogma.

80
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.275
According to the text, what may be the mark of an educated mind?

81
00:06:18.575 --> 00:06:25.642
A, certitude. B, the capacity for well-founded doubt. C, rationalism alone.

82
00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.821
The answer is B: the capacity for well-founded doubt.

83
00:06:35.317 --> 00:06:41.951
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is truth universal, or does it depend on culture?

84
00:06:47.251 --> 00:06:53.546
Part two: Use one inverted structure, defend a position and address one counter-argument.

85
00:06:59.846 --> 00:07:16.840
I'd argue that factual truth is universal, while moral judgements are more culturally shaped. Only through evidence can disputes about facts be settled. A relativist might object that evidence itself is interpreted culturally, but that does not make all interpretations equally valid.

86
00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:20.184
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:21.183 --> 00:07:27.156
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced inversion and emphasis.

88
00:07:27.456 --> 00:07:30.875
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:31.175 --> 00:07:33.641
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:33.941 --> 00:07:36.373
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

